# kernel-arc700.img -- Linux 4.19.56 for an ARC700 (ARCompact) core
#
# Runs on qemu-system-arc -M virt -cpu arc700 from the qemu fork
# (lordrasmus/qemu, branch uclibc-ng); upstream qemu has no ARC, and
# Synopsys' fork only decodes ARCv2/ARCv3.  The image is the plain vmlinux
# (ELF).
#
#   qemu-system-arc -M virt -cpu arc700 -m 256M -nographic \
#     -kernel kernel-arc700.img -initrd rootfs.img.xz
#
# -m 256M must match the kernel's memory: the device tree does not describe
# memory, so the kernel assumes 256 MiB at 0x80000000.  With more, qemu puts
# the initrd above what the kernel manages, and freeing it after unpacking
# ends in "BUG: Bad page state".
#
# The machine passes the initrd as "initrd=<start>,<size>" on the command
# line; the rest of the command line (earlycon, console=ttyS0) comes from the
# built-in device tree.
#
# Build: kernel-arc700.config is an ARC770 config with the built-in device
# tree qemu_arc700 and an empty INITRAMFS_SOURCE (the default initramfs brings
# /dev/console).  kernel-arc700-qemu-dts.patch adds that device tree: the 8250
# at 0xf0000000 on interrupt 24, as hw/arc/virt.c wires it, and a 50 MHz core
# clock, the rate the fork's timers count at (as for haps_hs.dts on ARCv2).
# A mismatch makes the guest clock run fast or slow by the same factor.
#
#   cd linux-4.19.56
#   patch -p1 < kernel-arc700-qemu-dts.patch
#   cp kernel-arc700.config .config
#   make ARCH=arc CROSS_COMPILE=arc-openadk-linux-uclibc- \
#        HOSTCFLAGS=-fcommon olddefconfig vmlinux
#
# The toolchain is the CI's arc700 toolchain (gcc 12.3.0).
